    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Collagen for women matters most at two points: the mid-twenties, when skin collagen
        starts falling by roughly 1% a year, and menopause, when oestrogen withdrawal takes about
        30% of it inside five years.</strong> The evidence is strongest for that second window.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Late 20s and 30s:</strong> the loss is real but slow. Sunscreen and protein intake do more for you here than any supplement.</li>
        <li><strong>40s (perimenopause):</strong> oestrogen begins swinging, skin gets drier and less springy, and hair shedding often starts. This is where most women first go looking.</li>
        <li><strong>Post-menopause:</strong> the best-studied window. Trials using 2.5g to 10g of collagen peptides daily improved skin elasticity, and 5g daily for 12 months improved bone mineral density in post-menopausal women.</li>
        <li><strong>The honest limit:</strong> collagen does not replace oestrogen, does not treat osteoporosis, and should not be started during pregnancy or breastfeeding without your doctor's clearance.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

