      <h2 id="the-curve">The curve nobody shows you</h2>

      <p>
        Collagen is the structural protein that makes up around a third of the protein in your body
        and roughly 75% of the dry weight of your skin. In the dermis it forms dense fibre bundles
        that hold the surface taut. When those bundles thin, everything sitting on top of them
        loses support.
      </p>

      <p>
        Most articles about collagen for women describe a gentle, even slope from 25 onwards. That
        is not what the measurements show. The baseline decline of about 1% per year is real, but
        it is overlaid by a second, much steeper curve that is specific to women and tied to
        oestrogen. Skin collagen content falls sharply in the years immediately surrounding
        menopause, then settles back to a slower rate. Two women of the same age can therefore be
        in very different places depending on where they sit relative to that transition.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is why age alone is a poor guide. In my OPD I see 44-year-olds who are already two
        years into perimenopause and 52-year-olds who are not, and their skin tells me before their
        history does. What follows is organised by decade because that is how women search for it,
        but the hormonal milestone matters more than the birthday.
      </p>

