      <h2 id="late-twenties">Your late twenties: when the drawdown starts</h2>

      <p>
        Collagen synthesis peaks in your early twenties and begins its slow decline from around 25.
        You will not see it. The loss in this decade is roughly 1% a year, which is far below what
        the eye can register, and almost everything you notice in your late twenties is surface
        level rather than structural.
      </p>

      <p>
        What actually changes here is repair speed. Skin that recovers from a late night in a day
        starts taking two. Post-acne marks sit around longer. Fine lines appear at the outer eye
        when you smile and then flatten out again, which is expression, not established creasing.
        The dermal scaffolding underneath is still largely intact.
      </p>

      <p>
        My honest position on supplementing at 27: it is optional, and it is not where your money
        works hardest. Daily broad-spectrum sunscreen protects against more collagen loss in this
        decade than any oral product can rebuild, because ultraviolet exposure activates the enzymes that
        cut collagen fibres apart. If you are choosing between the two, choose sunscreen. If you
        already wear it daily, a well-dosed collagen formula is a reasonable next step, and the
        argument for starting early is behavioural rather than biochemical: women who start a daily
        habit at 28 are usually still taking it at 45, which is when it counts most.
      </p>

      <p>
        If you want the full picture of what oral collagen does and does not do for skin at any
        age, that sits in a separate piece:
        <a href="/pages/collagen-for-skin">collagen for skin, and what the trial evidence actually shows</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="thirties">Your thirties: the first elasticity change</h2>

      <p>
        Your thirties are when the cumulative arithmetic starts to show. Ten years of 1% annual loss
        compounds into a visible difference in how quickly skin snaps back, and this is the decade
        when most women first describe their skin as looking tired rather than looking older.
      </p>

      <p>
        Three specific changes are typical. Skin retains less water, so it feels tight by evening.
        Pigmentation from earlier sun exposure surfaces as melasma or uneven tone, often triggered
        by pregnancy or hormonal contraception. And elasticity drops enough that the nasolabial
        folds and under-eye area hold their shape after you stop expressing. That last one is a
        genuine dermal change rather than a hydration issue.
      </p>

      <p>
        Pregnancy and breastfeeding also land in this decade for many women, and this is the one
        section where I want to be blunt. <strong>Do not start a collagen supplement while pregnant
        or breastfeeding without clearing it with your obstetrician.</strong> The collagen peptides
        themselves are food-derived protein and are not the concern. The concern is everything else
        in a modern beauty collagen formula: botanical extracts, high-dose antioxidants, herbal
        actives and vitamin levels that have never been tested in pregnancy. Absence of evidence is
        not safety data. Postpartum hair shedding is normal, hormonal and self-limiting, and it
        resolves on its own in most women by around six months without any supplement at all.
      </p>

