      <h2 id="forties">Your forties: perimenopause changes the rules</h2>

      <p>
        Perimenopause is the transition phase before periods stop, and it usually begins somewhere
        in the early to mid forties, lasting anywhere from two to eight years. Oestrogen does not
        decline smoothly through it. It swings, and skin responds to the swings.
      </p>

      <p>
        Oestrogen directly influences skin. It supports fibroblast activity (fibroblasts are the
        cells that manufacture collagen), it helps maintain hyaluronic acid content in the dermis,
        and it affects sebum production and skin thickness. When it becomes erratic, women report
        an odd combination: skin that is drier and duller than it has ever been, but also breaking
        out along the jaw the way it did at nineteen. Both are the same hormonal instability seen
        from different angles.
      </p>

      <p>
        Hair usually announces itself in this decade too. Diffuse thinning across the crown and a
        noticeably thinner ponytail are common perimenopausal complaints, and they are driven by
        follicles shortening their growth phase rather than by anything you are or are not eating.
        Collagen is not a treatment for that pattern, though it can support the surrounding
        conditions. I have written about where the line sits in
        <a href="/pages/collagen-for-hair">collagen for hair, and what it can and cannot do</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Practically, this is the decade where supplementation starts to earn its place. The loss is
        no longer 1% a year, dietary protein intake in most Indian women is already marginal, and
        the twelve-week timeline that collagen needs means starting before the steepest part of the
        curve is better than starting after it.
      </p>

      <h2 id="menopause">Is collagen for women worth it after menopause?</h2>

      <p>
        This is the window where the evidence is strongest, and my answer is a qualified yes. After
        menopause, skin collagen falls by roughly 30% in the first five years, and the loss in that
        period correlates more closely with years since menopause than with chronological age. A
        supplement that adds a modest amount back matters more against a steep decline than against
        a shallow one.
      </p>

      <p>
        Menopause is defined as twelve consecutive months without a period, and in Indian women it
        arrives earlier than the global average. Population data puts the median age at around 46
        to 48 years, roughly three to five years earlier than in Western cohorts. That means Indian
        women often spend a longer total stretch of life in the low-oestrogen state, which makes
        the post-menopausal decades a larger share of the problem than the global literature
        implies.
      </p>

      <p>
        What the trials support: the 2021 systematic review in the
        <em>International Journal of Dermatology</em>, pooling 19 randomised controlled trials
        across 1,125 participants (a majority of them women, many post-menopausal), found
        statistically significant improvements in skin hydration, elasticity and wrinkle depth with
        hydrolysed collagen versus placebo. Effective doses clustered between 2.5g and 10g daily,
        and elasticity changes showed up at 8 to 12 weeks rather than sooner.
      </p>

      <p>
        What the trials do not support, and I would rather say this plainly than sell around it:
        collagen is not hormone replacement. It does not address hot flushes, sleep disruption,
        mood changes, urogenital symptoms or the cardiovascular and bone risks that follow
        oestrogen withdrawal. If your symptoms are significant, the conversation to have is with a
        gynaecologist about menopausal hormone therapy, and a collagen supplement is an addition to
        that conversation rather than an alternative to it. Many of the skin trials were also
        industry-funded with modest sample sizes, which is worth holding in mind even where the
        direction of effect is consistent.
      </p>

      <h2 id="bone">Bone, not just skin</h2>

      <p>
        Here is the part of the post-menopausal story that gets left out of beauty collagen
        marketing. Bone is roughly 90% Type I collagen by protein content, with mineral deposited
        onto that protein framework. Oestrogen withdrawal accelerates bone loss for the same
        underlying reason it accelerates skin thinning.
      </p>

      <p>
        König and colleagues ran a randomised placebo-controlled trial in 131 post-menopausal women
        with reduced bone mineral density, published in <em>Nutrients</em> in 2018. Participants
        took 5g of specific collagen peptides daily for 12 months. The supplemented group showed
        significant increases in bone mineral density at the spine and femoral neck compared with
        placebo, alongside favourable changes in bone formation and degradation markers.
      </p>

      <p>
        That is one trial, in one population, at one dose, and it is not grounds for treating
        osteoporosis with a supplement. Osteoporosis is a diagnosed medical condition managed with
        DEXA scanning, prescription therapy where indicated, and correction of vitamin D and
        calcium status. What the trial does support is that collagen peptides act on bone tissue
        and not only on skin, which is a reasonable thing for a woman in her fifties to factor into
        which formula she picks.
      </p>

      <p>
        If joints and bones are your primary concern rather than skin, that belongs to a different
        formula and a different article. My co-founder, an orthopaedic surgeon, covers the
        cartilage and joint evidence in
        <a href="/pages/collagen-for-joint-pain">collagen for joint pain</a>, including the Type II
        collagen trials that are relevant to knees rather than to skin.
      </p>

      <h2 id="india">Why Indian women lose collagen faster</h2>

      <p>
        Two factors specific to India sit on top of the universal decline, and neither gets much
        attention in imported wellness content.
      </p>

      <p>
        The first is protein. Collagen synthesis needs amino acid substrate, and Indian diets are
        widely protein-short, particularly for vegetarian women who are also the ones most likely
        to be managing anaemia and low vitamin D. National dietary surveys consistently find average
        protein intake below recommended levels, and the gap is wider in women than in men because
        household food distribution tends not to favour them. A vegetarian meal built around dal,
        rice and a little paneer can be perfectly adequate in calories while falling well short in
        protein. Marine collagen is not vegetarian, which is a genuine limitation for a large share
        of Indian women, and I would rather they know that before ordering than after.
      </p>

      <p>
        The second is ultraviolet exposure. India sits at latitudes with high year-round UV index,
        and higher baseline melanin offers real but partial protection: it reduces burning far more
        than it reduces the long-wave UVA that drives collagen breakdown and pigmentation. Indian
        skin photoages differently from fair skin. It tends to hold structural firmness a little
        longer while showing pigmentary change much sooner, which is why so many Indian women in
        their forties describe the problem as dullness and dark patches rather than wrinkles.
      </p>

      <p>
        On cost, the arithmetic is worth doing before you buy. A daily collagen product in India
        typically runs somewhere between 30 and 100 rupees per serving. What decides value is not
        the headline price but the dose behind it. A 500mg sachet at 30 rupees is more expensive per
        studied gram than an 8,000mg shot at 90 rupees, because the sachet delivers a dose no trial
        has ever tested.
      </p>

