      <h2 id="choose">What is the best collagen for women at each stage?</h2>

      <p>
        The formula does not need to change much by decade. The dose and the co-factors matter far
        more than any age-targeted marketing, and most products sold as beauty collagen or glow
        collagen differ from each other in flavouring rather than in anything that reaches your
        dermis.
      </p>

      <p>Four checks, in the order they matter:</p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Is a milligram dose printed on the pack?</strong> Trials that found skin benefits
          used 2.5g to 10g of hydrolysed collagen peptides daily. If the label says "collagen blend"
          with no number, the number is small. This single check eliminates most of the market.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Is it hydrolysed?</strong> Whole collagen is far too large a molecule to survive
          digestion intact. Hydrolysis cleaves it into short peptides, some of which reach the
          bloodstream and appear to signal fibroblasts to increase output.
          <a href="/pages/collagen-peptides">Collagen peptides are explained in more detail here</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Are vitamin C and zinc in the formula?</strong> Collagen synthesis is enzymatic.
          Prolyl and lysyl hydroxylase both require vitamin C, and zinc activates enzymes on the
          building side while regulating the ones that break collagen down. Peptides without
          co-factors are bricks delivered to a site with no mortar.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>How much sugar per serving?</strong> Many collagen drinks carry 8g to 12g. Dietary
          sugar drives glycation, which stiffens and damages the collagen you already have, and that
          works directly against what you are paying for.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        For women whose priority is skin, hair and nails, a marine source is the sensible default
        because it is almost entirely Type I, the dominant collagen in the dermis, and it hydrolyses
        to a lower molecular weight than bovine. The comparison is laid out in
        <a href="/pages/marine-vs-bovine-collagen">marine vs bovine collagen</a>.
      </p>

