      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>

      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>At what age should a woman start taking collagen?</summary>
          <p>
            There is no evidence-backed starting age, but the biological argument gets stronger from
            the early forties, when perimenopausal oestrogen changes accelerate collagen loss beyond
            the baseline 1% a year. Starting in your late twenties is reasonable and harmless, though
            daily sunscreen will do more for your skin at that age than any supplement.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is collagen safe during pregnancy or breastfeeding?</summary>
          <p>
            Do not start one without your obstetrician's clearance. Collagen peptides are food-derived
            protein, but modern beauty collagen formulas also contain botanical extracts and
            high-dose antioxidants that have not been tested in pregnancy. Postpartum hair shedding
            is hormonal and usually resolves on its own by around six months.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does collagen help with menopause skin changes?</summary>
          <p>
            It helps with some of them. Trials using 2.5g to 10g of hydrolysed collagen daily
            improved skin hydration, elasticity and wrinkle depth, and post-menopausal women were
            well represented in that research. Collagen does not replace oestrogen and does nothing
            for hot flushes, sleep or mood, so speak to a gynaecologist about those.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What is the best collagen for women over 40?</summary>
          <p>
            Look for a marine Type I source at a dose between 2.5g and 10g daily, with vitamin C and
            zinc included and no added sugar. Collabloom delivers 8,000mg per 30ml shot with 250mg
            vitamin C from amla. Age-targeted labelling is marketing; the dose and the co-factors are
            what differ between products that work and products that do not.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can vegetarian women take collagen supplements?</summary>
          <p>
            Not marine or bovine collagen, both of which are animal-derived. There is no true
            vegetarian collagen, since the protein does not exist in plants, and vegan collagen
            builder products supply amino acids and vitamin C rather than collagen itself. If you are
            vegetarian, prioritise total daily protein from dal, paneer, curd and soy first.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How long does collagen take to work for women?</summary>
          <p>
            Hydration changes are often reported by week 4, while measurable elasticity improvement
            took 8 to 12 weeks in the published trials. Nails and hair sit somewhere in between,
            limited by how fast they physically grow. If you have taken it daily for twelve weeks and
            noticed nothing, it is fair to stop.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>

